1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:06,474
<font color="#000000">Deploying and training neural networks on edge
devices is often bottlenecked by tight memory.</font>

2
00:00:06,554 --> 00:00:11,877
<font color="#000000">Tensor rematerialization, recomputing intermediate
tensors instead of storing them,</font>

3
00:00:11,957 --> 00:00:14,676
<font color="#000000">trades extra compute for lower peak memory.</font>

4
00:00:14,756 --> 00:00:16,694
<font color="#000000">This paper introduces Moccasin,</font>

5
00:00:16,774 --> 00:00:23,724
<font color="#000000">a new constraint programming formulation for the
problem of minimizing execution time under a memory budget.</font>

6
00:00:23,804 --> 00:00:28,216
<font color="#000000">Unlike prior work that needs a quadratic number of
Boolean variables,</font>

7
00:00:28,296 --> 00:00:31,861
<font color="#000000">Moccasin uses only a linear number of integer variables,</font>

8
00:00:31,941 --> 00:00:37,720
<font color="#000000">letting it scale to much larger compute graphs and
run up to an order of magnitude faster.</font>

9
00:00:38,100 --> 00:00:43,021
<font color="#000000">Neural networks running on edge devices are
constrained above all by memory.</font>

10
00:00:43,101 --> 00:00:48,811
<font color="#000000">One way to fit a large model into a small memory
footprint is rematerialization: instead</font>

11
00:00:48,891 --> 00:00:51,246
<font color="#000000">of storing every intermediate tensor,</font>

12
00:00:51,326 --> 00:00:53,681
<font color="#000000">you recompute some of them on demand.</font>

13
00:00:53,761 --> 00:00:56,510
<font color="#000000">The catch is that recomputation costs time.</font>

14
00:00:56,590 --> 00:01:02,630
<font color="#000000">So the core problem is a scheduling one: for a
given compute graph and a fixed memory budget,</font>

15
00:01:02,710 --> 00:01:08,223
<font color="#000000">decide which tensors to retain and which to
recompute so that total execution time is</font>

16
00:01:08,303 --> 00:01:12,434
<font color="#000000">as small as possible while peak memory never
exceeds the budget.</font>

17
00:01:12,514 --> 00:01:17,040
<font color="#000000">This is a hard combinatorial optimization, in
general PSPACE-complete.</font>

18
00:01:17,420 --> 00:01:21,564
<font color="#000000">The leading prior method, Checkmate,</font>

19
00:01:21,644 --> 00:01:25,789
<font color="#000000">casts rematerialization as a mixed-integer linear program.</font>

20
00:01:25,869 --> 00:01:30,013
<font color="#000000">It is expressive,</font>

21
00:01:30,093 --> 00:01:34,238
<font color="#000000">but its number of Boolean decision variables grows
with the square of the number of nodes in the graph.</font>

22
00:01:34,318 --> 00:01:38,462
<font color="#000000">That quadratic growth becomes a wall: for graphs
with a few hundred nodes and a few thousand edges,</font>

23
00:01:38,542 --> 00:01:42,687
<font color="#000000">Checkmate either times out or runs out of memory
during the solve.</font>

24
00:01:42,767 --> 00:01:46,911
<font color="#000000">Because the trend toward on-device training and
stricter latency targets keeps enlarging these graphs,</font>

25
00:01:46,991 --> 00:01:51,136
<font color="#000000">we need a formulation whose complexity grows much
more slowly,</font>

26
00:01:51,216 --> 00:01:55,360
<font color="#000000">so realistic graphs can be solved within an
acceptable compile time.</font>

27
00:01:55,740 --> 00:02:00,117
<font color="#FFFFFF">The paper's main contribution is Moccasin,</font>

28
00:02:00,197 --> 00:02:04,574
<font color="#FFFFFF">a constraint programming formulation of tensor
rematerialization.</font>

29
00:02:04,654 --> 00:02:09,031
<font color="#FFFFFF">Its central idea is to represent each node's
decisions as a small number of retention intervals,</font>

30
00:02:09,111 --> 00:02:13,488
<font color="#FFFFFF">defined by the start and end event of the tensor's
lifetime,</font>

31
00:02:13,568 --> 00:02:17,945
<font color="#FFFFFF">which reduces the count of discrete variables from
quadratic to linear in the number of nodes.</font>

32
00:02:18,025 --> 00:02:22,402
<font color="#FFFFFF">On top of this,</font>

33
00:02:22,482 --> 00:02:26,859
<font color="#FFFFFF">the authors show how to encode the nonlinear memory
and precedence constraints using standard CP building blocks,</font>

34
00:02:26,939 --> 00:02:31,316
<font color="#FFFFFF">the cumulative and reservoir constraints,</font>

35
00:02:31,396 --> 00:02:35,773
<font color="#FFFFFF">and they add an optional variant that enforces an
input topological ordering to further reduce the search space.</font>

36
00:02:35,853 --> 00:02:40,230
<font color="#FFFFFF">The payoff is up to an order-of-magnitude speedup
over prior work on large graphs.</font>

37
00:02:40,610 --> 00:02:44,161
<font color="#000000">Moccasin represents the schedule with retention intervals.</font>

38
00:02:44,241 --> 00:02:45,851
<font color="#000000">For each node in the graph,</font>

39
00:02:45,931 --> 00:02:49,669
<font color="#000000">the method allows up to a small constant number of
intervals,</font>

40
00:02:49,749 --> 00:02:53,801
<font color="#000000">each specified by an integer start event and an
integer end event,</font>

41
00:02:53,881 --> 00:02:59,184
<font color="#000000">indicating when that node's output tensor is
computed and how long it stays in memory.</font>

42
00:02:59,264 --> 00:03:03,065
<font color="#000000">A Boolean flag marks whether each interval is
actually active,</font>

43
00:03:03,145 --> 00:03:06,633
<font color="#000000">so a node need not be recomputed a fixed number of times.</font>

44
00:03:06,713 --> 00:03:09,325
<font color="#000000">The objective minimizes the total duration,</font>

45
00:03:09,405 --> 00:03:14,896
<font color="#000000">a weighted sum over the active compute events where
the weights are node execution times.</font>

46
00:03:14,976 --> 00:03:20,342
<font color="#000000">The memory budget is enforced through the
constraint programming cumulative constraint,</font>

47
00:03:20,422 --> 00:03:24,661
<font color="#000000">treating tensor sizes as resource demands and the
budget as capacity,</font>

48
00:03:24,741 --> 00:03:31,046
<font color="#000000">while data dependencies between predecessors and
successors are handled with the reservoir constraint.</font>

49
00:03:31,126 --> 00:03:34,802
<font color="#000000">The whole model is handed to Google OR-Tools'
CP-SAT solver.</font>

50
00:03:34,882 --> 00:03:38,871
<font color="#000000">Because each node uses only a small constant number
of intervals,</font>

51
00:03:38,951 --> 00:03:43,190
<font color="#000000">the total number of integer variables stays linear
in the graph size.</font>

52
00:03:43,570 --> 00:03:46,438
<font color="#000000">The evaluation uses a range of compute graphs.</font>

53
00:03:46,518 --> 00:03:50,989
<font color="#000000">The main scaling study is on four random layered
graphs, G1 through G4,</font>

54
00:03:51,069 --> 00:03:56,437
<font color="#000000">whose sizes grow from one hundred nodes and a
couple hundred edges up to one thousand</font>

55
00:03:56,517 --> 00:03:58,744
<font color="#000000">nodes and nearly six thousand edges.</font>

56
00:03:58,824 --> 00:04:04,512
<font color="#000000">There is also a real-world compute graph with four
hundred forty-two nodes and over twelve</font>

57
00:04:04,592 --> 00:04:07,525
<font color="#000000">hundred edges used for the headline comparison.</font>

58
00:04:07,605 --> 00:04:13,165
<font color="#000000">For every graph the memory budget is set to eighty
and ninety percent of the peak memory</font>

59
00:04:13,245 --> 00:04:16,369
<font color="#000000">of the initial schedule without rematerialization,</font>

60
00:04:16,449 --> 00:04:19,830
<font color="#000000">so the solver is forced to actually recompute tensors.</font>

61
00:04:19,910 --> 00:04:25,150
<font color="#000000">All experiments run on a sixteen-core workstation
with thirty-two gigabytes of RAM.</font>

62
00:04:25,530 --> 00:04:29,379
<font color="#000000">Across the board,</font>

63
00:04:29,459 --> 00:04:33,308
<font color="#000000">Moccasin solves the rematerialization problem
substantially faster than Checkmate,</font>

64
00:04:33,388 --> 00:04:37,237
<font color="#000000">up to an order of magnitude on the larger graphs.</font>

65
00:04:37,317 --> 00:04:41,166
<font color="#000000">On the smallest graph the two are comparable, but
the gap widens quickly.</font>

66
00:04:41,246 --> 00:04:45,095
<font color="#000000">For the graph with two hundred fifty nodes and a
tight memory budget,</font>

67
00:04:45,175 --> 00:04:49,025
<font color="#000000">Checkmate fails to find any feasible solution
within thirty minutes,</font>

68
00:04:49,105 --> 00:04:52,954
<font color="#000000">and at the loosest budget it takes ten minutes,
while Moccasin finishes in seconds.</font>

69
00:04:53,034 --> 00:04:56,883
<font color="#000000">For the five hundred and one thousand node graphs,
Checkmate times out entirely,</font>

70
00:04:56,963 --> 00:05:00,812
<font color="#000000">finding no solution even given three hours, and it
exits with an out-of-memory error,</font>

71
00:05:00,892 --> 00:05:04,741
<font color="#000000">whereas Moccasin converges to a good,</font>

72
00:05:04,821 --> 00:05:08,670
<font color="#000000">low-duration-increase solution in under an hour.</font>

73
00:05:09,050 --> 00:05:11,127
<font color="#FFFFFF">The paper studies a few key knobs.</font>

74
00:05:11,207 --> 00:05:16,647
<font color="#FFFFFF">The most important is the optional
topological-ordering restriction: enforcing an input</font>

75
00:05:16,727 --> 00:05:21,976
<font color="#FFFFFF">ordering enlarges the variable domain slightly but
shrinks the overall search space,</font>

76
00:05:22,056 --> 00:05:26,227
<font color="#FFFFFF">reducing solve time compared to the fully
unrestricted formulation.</font>

77
00:05:26,307 --> 00:05:31,493
<font color="#FFFFFF">The per-node interval budget, called C, is fixed at
two throughout the experiments,</font>

78
00:05:31,573 --> 00:05:34,285
<font color="#FFFFFF">which the authors note in every plot legend.</font>

79
00:05:34,365 --> 00:05:39,995
<font color="#FFFFFF">The complexity table makes the contrast concrete:
Checkmate's Boolean variable count grows</font>

80
00:05:40,075 --> 00:05:43,738
<font color="#FFFFFF">quadratically in the number of nodes plus a
node-edge term,</font>

81
00:05:43,818 --> 00:05:49,004
<font color="#FFFFFF">while Moccasin's integer variable count grows only
linearly in the number of nodes,</font>

82
00:05:49,084 --> 00:05:50,590
<font color="#FFFFFF">with a constant factor C.</font>

83
00:05:50,970 --> 00:05:54,812
<font color="#000000">A few numbers capture the impact.</font>

84
00:05:54,892 --> 00:05:58,734
<font color="#000000">Moccasin needs only a linear number of integer
variables in the graph size,</font>

85
00:05:58,814 --> 00:06:02,656
<font color="#000000">compared to a quadratic number of Boolean variables
for the prior state of the art.</font>

86
00:06:02,736 --> 00:06:06,578
<font color="#000000">This translates into solve times up to an order of
magnitude, roughly ten times,</font>

87
00:06:06,658 --> 00:06:10,500
<font color="#000000">faster on large graphs.</font>

88
00:06:10,580 --> 00:06:14,422
<font color="#000000">And the solutions it finds are high quality: the
total duration increase from rematerialization</font>

89
00:06:14,502 --> 00:06:18,344
<font color="#000000">stays consistently below five percent.</font>

90
00:06:18,424 --> 00:06:22,266
<font color="#000000">Crucially,</font>

91
00:06:22,346 --> 00:06:26,188
<font color="#000000">Moccasin scales to graphs with up to one thousand
nodes and nearly six thousand edges,</font>

92
00:06:26,268 --> 00:06:30,110
<font color="#000000">a regime where the competing method simply times
out or runs out of memory.</font>

93
00:06:30,490 --> 00:06:35,883
<font color="#000000">The lasting takeaway is that the right problem
formulation changes what is tractable.</font>

94
00:06:35,963 --> 00:06:42,580
<font color="#000000">By expressing rematerialization decisions as a
small set of retention intervals in a constraint program,</font>

95
00:06:42,660 --> 00:06:48,568
<font color="#000000">Moccasin reduces the number of discrete variables
from quadratic to linear in the graph size.</font>

96
00:06:48,648 --> 00:06:54,621
<font color="#000000">That single change lets it scale to compute graphs
far larger than prior methods could handle,</font>

97
00:06:54,701 --> 00:07:00,802
<font color="#000000">solving them up to ten times faster while keeping
the added runtime overhead under five percent.</font>

98
00:07:00,882 --> 00:07:04,215
<font color="#000000">And because the output is just an execution sequence,</font>

99
00:07:04,295 --> 00:07:07,370
<font color="#000000">the resulting schedule can run on any CPU or GPU.</font>
